      "notes": "NEKO MAKER 2: Starlit Girl is a fantasy life-sim / narrative game. Some themes may not be suitable for younger players.\r\n\r\nViolence\r\nIncludes mild, non-realistic fantasy violence, such as sword training, tournament matches, and school conflicts. Combat is resolved through stats and story scenes. No blood, gore, killing, or death depictions. A few events mention minor scrapes from training, or optional responses to bullying. Tone is generally cartoon-like / fantasy.\r\n\r\nSexual content\r\nNo sexual acts, nudity, pornography, or sexual violence. Some storylines involve teen romance and confessions (crushes, confessions, rejection) and familial emotional bonds between guardian and ward. These are narrative only, not sexual in nature.\r\n\r\nPotentially sensitive themes\r\n\r\nEmotional stress, burnout, trust issues, and mental/emotional breakdown; some endings involve the protagonist needing rest and professional care. No depictions of suicide or self-harm.\r\nSchool bullying and peer exclusion.\r\nMild unease or mystery (nightmares, foggy ruins, star-mark anomalies). Not a horror game; no jump scares.\r\nRare epilogue text mentions adults sharing wine in a social context. No alcohol abuse or drug use.\r\nSome routes involve moral choices and morally gray errands; no explicit depictions of drugs or trafficking.\r\nLanguage\r\nOccasional mild colloquial language in some localizations.\r\n\r\nOther\r\nSingle-player narrative only. No gambling, real-money purchases, or player chat. Some visual assets were created with generative AI during development and shipped as pre-made in-game art (not generated live during play).\r\n\r\nPlayers sensitive to emotional distress, parent\u2013child themes, or teen romance may wish to review screenshots and the trailer before purchasing."
